xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:20:  **Printed Notation 3.5 is P-ambiguous.** Verbatim p.12: “Assume \(F=I_P(u)\) for some \(P\) … Set \(\kappa_F:=\kappa/e_j\), where \(\alpha_j\le u<\alpha_{j+1}\).” Two presentations of the same vertex can (and in case III do) give two values. The lattice identities (1) are the definitions: adjoining \(P\)’s jump at \(v\) multiplies the common index \(L_0\) by \(\nu_G\), so \(\kappa_G=\nu_G L_0\); \(Q\)’s jump at \(u\) multiplies it by \(\nu_F\), so \(\kappa_F(Q)=\nu_F L_0\).
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:30:  **Statement 3.8 as printed is general.** Verbatim p.14: “For any polynomial \(h(x,y)\), and for any \(F\in V_a\), one has \(D_{h,F}\in\mathbb Z\).” It is not restricted to Keller pairs or to book cells. Notation 3.10 defines \(d_{h,F}\) as the actual leading exponent in a suitable chart; Notation 3.11 sets \(D_{h,F}:=\kappa_F d_{h,F}\). The chart \(y=\phi_{<u}(x)+x^{-u}\eta\) is presentation-independent (shared coefficients below \(u\)). Every exponent of \(h^F\) lies on the group generated by \(L_0^{-1}\mathbb Z\) and \(u\in(\nu_F L_0)^{-1}\mathbb Z\), hence \(d_{h,F}\in(\nu_F L_0)^{-1}\mathbb Z\). Q makes \(D_{h,F}\) integral; P gives \(D_{h,F}\in\nu_F^{-1}\mathbb Z\), not integral in general.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:39:  **Proposition 5.5 independently forces Q at poles.** Printed p.26 boxed (18): \(\Lambda(P)=D_{g,F}/\nu_F\) if \(c=0\), and \(=D_{g,F}\) if \(c\neq 0\). Printed \(c=0\) proof (p.27): \(P\) has \(\alpha_m<u\); any other \(Q\) realizing \(F\) has characteristics \((\nu_F\kappa,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_m,u)\); then \(\Lambda(P)=\kappa d_{g,F}=D_F/\nu_F\). Together with \(D_{g,F}=\kappa_F d_{g,F}\) this is \(\kappa_F=\nu_F\kappa\), the Q-value. P-value \(\kappa_F=\kappa\) would make the displayed equality \(\kappa d=(\kappa d)/\nu_F\), false at a pole. The dropped \(g\)-subscripts are the already audited Proposition 5.5 typo; boxed (18) carries the correct subscript. Scope is exactly the in-context \(c=0\) pole case the note claims — it does **not** by itself force Q at a nonpole class-C merge. The note already records that caveat.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:51:  What *can* be said about a hypothetical realization of a book class-C merge: the merge is doubly realized by definition of case III / class C, N1 gives \(\gcd(\bar\kappa^Q,\nu)=1\), and (1c) then gives \(\bar\kappa^P=\bar\kappa^Q/\nu\) with exact denominator \(\nu\ge 2\). That already contradicts the thesis-wide integer decoration (Statement 9.4, every printed Q-datum) without needing a polynomial. For Statement 3.8 itself one still needs a concrete \(d_{h,F}\); the model supplies one, the books do not.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:110:  **No other printed/promoted equality theorem in the audited chain.** SHEET6-III files E5 as an erratum and records the printed-literal alternative \(\nu_F=\nu_G\) as a *reading*, not a derivation. HIII-REVIEW Front 3 confirms both coherent readings give the same \(\lambda\)-bound and that “no printed computation discriminates.” SIGRAY-AUDIT upgrades H5a via Statement 3.8; it does not mention index equality. BOOK-OFFAXIS §11a records both books and does not prove the restriction. N1, AF2, P0/P3, St 8.2/8.4/8.5 (8.5 is inapplicable because the case-III parent lies in \(V_{2,a}\)), St 5.2, and St 6.2 do not force \(\nu_F=\nu_G\).
