+## 2. D23: the exact objects and the wrong-object boundary
+
+There are four distinct objects in play.
+
+1. `directionb_core23_p105337.ms` is the 87-variable unspecialized modular
+   D23 hybrid.  It retains `A1,A2,HW1,HW2` and their radical/guard data.  This
+   is the modular object closest to the full finite-radical chart.  Its long
+   extension was shed without a verdict.
+2. `directionb_row22compat_p*.ms` is 72 variables and 54 equations, but
+   `cases/directionb_row22_emit.py` calls `FC.radical_point(p)` and substitutes
+   one cube root for each `Ai` and one sign for each `HWi/Wi`.  It is one of
+   the `3*3*2*2=36` radical fibers.
+3. `directionb_row22red_p*.ms` is the exact 29-variable pivot-eliminated form
+   of that same fixed radical fiber.  Its variety is the 72v compatibility
+   variety times the recorded affine pivot factor in the direction appropriate
+   to the reconstruction; it is not equivalent to the unspecialized 87v
+   object.
+4. `directionb_fiber_gb_p105337.out.txt` is the reduced grevlex GB of D21 on
+   that representative radical fiber.  The tracked local artifact exists only
+   for `p=105337`.
+
+The phrase “fiber GB” is easy to misread.  It is **not** a GB at one fixed D21
+base point.  It describes the whole 13-dimensional D21 locus after the finite
+radical coordinates have been specialized.  Conversely, it is not a
+parametric GB whose 13 directions are coefficient parameters: those directions
+are ordinary quotient-ring variables.  It is also not a 397-dimensional
+vector-space basis and not a list of 397 points.
+
+The exact banked ring is
+
+```text
+R0 = F_105337[
+ x68,x70,x71,x72,x73,x47,x52,x53,x54,x55,x57,x58,x59,x60,
+ x62,x63,x65,x66,W1,W2,uW1,uW2
+]
+```
+
+in precisely that grevlex variable order.  The 397 polynomials contain
+282,304 terms.  `A1,A2` and the ratios `HWi/Wi` have been specialized, while
+`W1,W2` remain symbolic and invertible through `uWi*Wi-1`.  The basis pins
+quartics for `W1,W2`; therefore it covers all 16 pole-scale root branches over
+the algebraic closure inside this radical fiber, not just one `W` point.
+
+Consequently, a current `[1]` would be important but fiber-local.  It would not
+kill the other 35 radical fibers, the conjugate `sqrt(3)` embedding absent a
+proved transport, characteristic zero, B-unfrozen residue A, or JC2.  Equal
+support and equal census on the 36 D21 fibers do not prove ideal isomorphism.
+For EMPTY, either enumerate the full cover or prove an explicit symmetry that
+transports the equations and saturation guards.
+
+## 3. The NF quick shot: exact result and best software move
+
+### 3.1 Which rows can be reduced
+
+The six raw rows of the 72v compatibility object do not live in `R0`: they
+still contain the 22 pivot coordinates.  Reducing them directly against the
+397-element basis is a wrong-ring operation.
+
+The correct targets are equations 24--29 (zero-based) of
+`directionb_row22red_p105337.ms`.  They live in
+
+```text
+S = R0[x16,x17,x24,x25,x32,x37,x69].
+```
+
+The banked GB embeds canonically in `S` and remains a GB because the extended
+grevlex order restricts to the banked order on `R0`.  The seven new variables
+must remain polynomial variables; treating them as a coefficient field would
+silently invert nonunits and change emptiness.
+
+### 3.2 What a constant NF would prove
+
+Let `I21=<G>` and let `c_i` be one correct post-pivot compatibility row.  If
+
+```text
+NF_G(c_i) = lambda,       lambda in F_105337^*,
+```
+
+then `c_i-lambda` lies in `I21*S`, so
+
+```text
+I21*S + <c_i> = S.
+```
+
+Thus even one nonzero constant NF would prove that the compatibility
+intersection is empty on the **entire representative fixed-radical D21
+locus**, uniformly in all seven auxiliary variables and all symbolic `W`
+branches.  A zero NF would say that row is redundant.  A nonconstant NF says
+nothing alone: several nonconstant remainders may jointly generate 1, or may
+have a large common zero set.
+
+Even a constant would not prove the full D23 kill for the scope reasons in
+Section 2.  Repeating it at three primes would still be modular evidence, not
+an exact characteristic-zero identity.
+
+### 3.3 Actual `p=105337` result
+
+The quick shot is no longer hypothetical.  Exact Singular reduction and
+msolve's known-basis NF mode agree, with all 397 listed leading monomials
+checked against the declared grevlex order.
+
+- None of the six NFs is zero or a nonzero constant.
+- Each row falls from 1,216/1,218 terms and degree 9 to exactly 150 terms and
+  degree 5.
+- All dependence on `x16,x24,x69` disappears.  The other four auxiliary
+  variables occur only through
+
+  ```text
+  d1 = x17-x32,       d2 = x25-x37.
+  ```
+
+- The `6 x 2` coefficient matrix of `(d1,d2)` has rank two everywhere on the
+  chart.  Rows 0 and 1 have minor
+
+  ```text
+  76716*uW1^2*uW2^2,
+  ```
+
+  a unit modulo `uWi*Wi-1`.  There is no determinantal rank-drop boundary on
+  this prime/fiber.
+- Eliminating `d1,d2` initially gives four 10-term, degree-4 core rows
+  `R2,...,R5`, but they satisfy the exact constant syzygy
+
+  ```text
+  77149*R2 + 16527*R3 + 81917*R4 + R5 = 0  (mod 105337).
+  ```
+
+  Their constant row rank is three.
+- Each obstruction is a linear form in only six features:
+
+  ```text
+  (x57-x65)^2*uW1^2,  (x57-x65)^2*uW2^2,
+  x53*uW1,             x58*uW2,             x70, x72.
+  ```
+
+  Notably, `x53,x58,x70,x72` all occur in the banked 13-variable free set.
+  This is not a proof of properness, but it strengthens the NONEMPTY prior.
+
+Therefore, on this prime and radical fiber,
+
+```text
+V(row22red) is empty
+  iff V(I21 + <R2,R3,R4>) is empty.
+```
+
+The five auxiliary directions invisible to the NFs are free; `d1,d2` are
+then recovered uniquely from the first two NFs.  Pivots and the four solved
+deep directions are recovered afterward, with the six deep-kernel directions
+remaining free as prescribed by the rank-four Schur step.
+
+### 3.4 Acceleration ratings
+
+| emission/workflow | rating | reason |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Ordinary solver input: 397 GB polynomials + six unreduced rows | **3/10** | About 290k input terms; an ordinary ideal solve may recompute the GB and all critical-pair structure.  Merely listing a GB is not a warm start. |
+| Known-basis normal forms (`-n 6 -i`, after a toy/version gate) | **10/10** | This is the correct quick shot and completed locally in 0.76 s; Singular independently agrees. |
+| True incremental solve from `G` plus three 10-term rows | **8/10** | Algebraically right, but engine support matters; a classical Singular first increment did not finish inside a three-minute pilot. |
+| **Original 22v D21 fiber core + three 10-term rows** | **10/10, preferred** | Only 29 equations and **5,378 total terms**.  It lets the engine recover the known 397-element D21 basis from the 5,348-term core, previously an 11 s task, without ingesting the 10 MB basis. |
+
+The headline “GB warm start” should therefore be refined:
+
+> Use the banked GB to discover and certify the quotient rows; solve the
+> sparse original D21 generators plus the three quotient obstructions.
+
+Before this becomes a promoted emission, retain division/provenance data for
+`c_i-NF(c_i)`, verify the GB against the original D21 ideal, independently
+reconstruct all six compatibility rows, repeat at the other two primes, and
+repeat on every radical fiber or prove symmetry.  The current GB has no
+cofactor transformation back to the 24 D21 input rows, so the present result
+is an exact modular discovery and solver target, not yet a global proof
+certificate.
